## Before class

This is a **lab**. There's no new reading. There's prep, and the lab only works if you bring it, because an author who can't push leaves their reviewer with nothing to review.

1. **Bring a charged laptop** with your development environment running. You'll be in a terminal and on GitHub for the whole hour.
2. **Make sure you can push** to your project's repository, not just clone it. Accepting the invitation isn't the same as having pushed once. Run `git push --dry-run` in your clone tonight: "Everything up-to-date" means you're ready.
3. **Working solo? Add your review partner** to your repository: **Settings** → **Collaborators and teams** → **Add people**, role **Write**. They need access to be requested as your reviewer, and you need it on theirs.
4. **Skim the <course-link type="page" id="pg-git-lab-guide">Git Workflow Lab Guide</course-link>.** It walks the whole lab step by step, with every command. Read Part 4 closely, and come with your leanings on your project's **six git standards**.
5. **Finish your design document revisions** before class, or nearly. They're due end of day today too, and lab time isn't revision time.

**Due end of day today:** <course-link type="assignment" id="cp5">CP5 — Design Document (revised)</course-link> and <course-link type="assignment" id="cp6">CP6 — Git Workflow Lab</course-link>. CP6 happens in the lab itself.

